WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.740
Near Cambridge, birds took dark peppered moths more often than pale ones.

2
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.720
The moth has a pale form, called typica, and a dark form, called carbonaria.

3
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.280
Michael Majerus released moths there over six years.

4
00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:19.100
Cook and colleagues analysed his records.

5
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:25.040
A dark moth survived each day at about 0.91 times the rate of a pale one.

6
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.460
What does that single number do to a wood full of moths?

7
00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:29.450
... ...

8
00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:31.950
Try an exercise with invented numbers.

9
00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:36.550
A wood holds 1,000 pale moths and 1,000 dark moths.

10
00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:40.270
Say pale moths survive each day at 0.80.

11
00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:44.790
Assume no migration, no reproduction and no change in rates.

12
00:00:45.783 --> 00:00:50.003
Dark moths survive at 0.909 times the pale rate.

13
00:00:50.803 --> 00:00:55.443
So dark daily survival is 0.80 times 0.909.

14
00:00:56.703 --> 00:01:05.083
That is 0.7272. After one day, 800 pale moths are expected, and 727.2 dark.

15
00:01:05.923 --> 00:01:07.983
That last figure is an expected count.

16
00:01:08.783 --> 00:01:11.623
A fraction of a moth is an expectation, not a moth.

17
00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:14.987
Now ask for the dark share among the survivors.

18
00:01:16.447 --> 00:01:22.707
727.2 out of 1,527.2 is 0.476.

19
00:01:24.067 --> 00:01:28.407
The dark share falls from 50% % to 47.6%.

20
00:01:28.407 --> 00:01:31.107
The pale rate and the starting thousands cancel.

21
00:01:32.147 --> 00:01:33.227
Only the ratio matters.

22
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.380
The ratio compounds from day to day.

23
00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:42.460
Each extra day multiplies the dark to pale ratio by 0.909 again.

24
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:46.360
After five days that ratio is 0.621.

25
00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:49.480
The dark share is then about 38%.

26
00:01:50.433 --> 00:01:57.253
Keep the limits. The 0.091 is relative and per day, at one site over those years.

27
00:01:57.833 --> 00:02:02.973
It is not an annual decline, not an absolute death rate, and not a figure for all of

28
00:02:02.973 --> 00:02:09.253
Britain. Majoris's moths also disappeared, and only about 26 % of disappearances

29
00:02:09.253 --> 00:02:10.853
were witnessed bird attacks.

30
00:02:11.753 --> 00:02:16.033
A change in morph share is not the same number as a change in allele frequency.

31
00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:21.433
A relative daily survival compounds into a change in morph share, at one site.
